      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Bell's Theorem, also known as Bell's inequality, is a fundamental concept in quantum mechanics that challenges classical notions of reality. It states that any local hidden variable theory, which attempts to explain quantum phenomena without relying on non-local communication, must violate the inequality. The inequality is mathematically expressed as B = p*c - x^2, where p is the probability of an event, c is the speed of light, and x is the difference between the measurement outcomes. \n\nTo derive the inequality, suppose we have two independent systems A and B, each with state A(i) and B(j) and corresponding measurements λ_A and λ_B. We can assume a local hypothesis that the outcome is determined by two hidden variables, a and b, which take on any value (i, j). The logic behind Bell's theorem is based on the classically unthinkable idea that each hidden variable is connected to a specific outcome of a measurement. To calculate the probability P of this scenario, we take the absolute value of the products of two expressions representing possible measurement plottings of the outcome. \n\nThese expressions evaluate the amount of contributions due to the intermediate (classical) operation to correctly calculate overall absolute probabilities P. By assuming the evidence favors the",
